in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x+5,\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,x+3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287718 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b635e983-fa39-5831-8b68-a64de13e9b19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287718 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=-x+5$ and *below* the line $y=x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<-x+5,\\,y<x+3.\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<-x+5,\\,y<x+3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287722 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287722 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287722 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287722 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287722 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287722 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/67/67eeabfcff4eb24e14c15691c1c9de746a8870808197739577f7696faeada5bc.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,4,\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,4x+1}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287722 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db5f24a8-6bb9-526d-89dd-0ad32386f5f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287722 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=4$ and *below* the line $y=4x+1.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<4,\\,y<4x+1.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=4$ and $y=4x+1$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le 4,\\,y\\le 4x+1\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le 4,\\,y\\le 4x+1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c7/c7f7b1dc9bf4da61b573345aaf92fe0d29ea00ef5558a9fca985d8af25fa0a03.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,2\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53ac5dfb-f75e-5ef2-a84c-e58da3b6698e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>2.\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>2\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409894 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409894 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409894 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53607ff6-5913-50ea-923c-e269930ccd31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409895 #edb/ref 1080 "<" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409895 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409895 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d74e258e-f0dd-579c-9148-3c83bfa32b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409896 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409896 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409896 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d9099832-9536-5764-b8f9-82d9f50c96e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409897 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409897 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409897 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409897 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409897 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1979d639-7c7b-51e8-9e83-40721047de2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409898 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409898 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409898 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409898 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409898 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "38cdacac-1881-5ec8-b9e9-d206d7013904" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9a1c0edf-ff99-50f4-9ecf-942f2ee605d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/59/592dc9d0d4713406cf9bf9c6ad966cb4446420113aa0e94fcfdcdb35b6471a9c.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$144$\n\n$\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6164b43d-cb18-5fe7-a896-e7c6ff8846af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=144.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,144.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref